@0.5, 6
MikuMikuTelop Practical Guide

:get def

@+1
Whether you watched the previous introduction or not,
welcome to the MikuMikuTelop practical guide.

@+1
In this video, we will introduce a subtitle effect creation tool
and explain the specific usage of MikuMikuTelop.


@+1
※Note

The latest version at the time of this video's release is 1.2.
I will explain it together with the new features,
so please make sure to update.
Previous scripts can still be used as-is.

:get title

@+2, 6
1. Introduction

:get def

@+1
First, I must mention that
MikuMikuTelop does not provide
the kind of powerful subtitles that video editing software offers.

@+1
Rather, by synchronizing motion and subtitle timing,
you can accomplish things in MMD simply,
apply effects to subtitles,
place subtitles in 3D space,
and other needs that are difficult to meet with video editing software
— this tool may be able to fulfill them.

:get title

@+1, 5
2. How to Use the Tool

:get def

@+1
Simply drag and drop a file containing MMTScript.
In the same folder as that file,
a folder named (filename)_MMT will be created,
containing texture and effect files.

Then, load the effect accessory in MMD
and the subtitles will be displayed.

@+1
MikuMikuTelop also has an auto-update feature like MME.
When you update a script file registered in the list,
the effect is also updated.
MME further detects this and
immediately reflects it in the MMD display.

:get title

@+2, 6
3. Editing Scripts

:get def

@+1
Basically, it's no different from a regular text file,
so you can edit it with Notepad or other editors.

Load the sample into MikuMikuTelop, display it in MMD
to confirm it works, then try editing it
and see how the displayed text changes.


@+1
One reason for choosing the script format is that
for long texts like in this video,
you can write them in your preferred editor.

Personally, my favorite is EmEditor.
I also use it for writing effects.

:get title

@+2, 6
4. Panel Mode

:get def

@+1
Check the "Shadow" checkbox of the accessory
to enable self-shadow for the effect accessory.

@+1
Then, the subtitle display screen
will be placed in 3D space like a flat panel.

As an accessory, it can also be moved and rotated.

:billboard

@+1,9
Billboard display is also available.

:pos_y +-20
:pos_x +-30

@*0
Each subtitle faces the camera direction.

:billboard_off

:get title

@+2, 6
5. MMTScript - Text Commands

:get def

@+1
Now let's briefly explain the script.
Commands for displaying subtitles
are called "Text Commands."


@+1
They are written as:
"@" (at mark) + time expression + "," (comma) + duration expression
For example,
@@ 10, 5
looks like this.

Text following a text command
will be displayed as a subtitle.

@+1
The standard unit is seconds, but adding "F" after a number
allows you to specify frames.

Also, adding "+" (plus) before the time expression
sets the time relative to the end of the previous subtitle,
and adding "*" (asterisk)
sets the time relative to the start of the previous subtitle.


@+1
If you omit the duration, it will be
automatically determined based on the number of characters.

:get title

@+2, 6
6. MMTScript - System Commands

:get def

@+1
System commands include formatting and other settings
that control how subtitles are displayed.


@+1
The basic format is:
":" (colon) + command name
followed by parameters on the next line(s).
Some commands don't require parameters.

:size 20
:fontcolor 255,200,230
:bold
:italic
:underline
:pos 0, 50
:align_center
:valign_top
:edge 12
:edgecolor 0,30,80
:edge_round
:margin 25,25
:backcolor 100,255,0,30

@+1
By using system commands,
you can create formatting like this...

:get def

:fontcolor 230,255,200
:edge 0
:shadow 8
:shadowcolor 70,0,0
:shadowblur 8
:tate
:fade 2
:size 14

@+1
Adjusting the fade-in time,
I also added a vertical writing feature.

:get def

@+1
There's a lot more that I can't cover here,
but please look through the sample and specification document
and try it out for yourself.


:align_left
:valign_top
:pos -90,90
:size 10
:fade 1

